<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Auto Backup on API 28–30. API 31+ uses xml/data_extraction_rules.xml, and
    minSdk is 28, so both files are live and both have to say the same thing.

    This shipped as the scaffolding template: every rule commented out, which
    under android:allowBackup="true" means *everything* the app writes is copied
    to the user's cloud backup. That was survivable only by accident — the app
    stores four presentation integers and nothing else — and RedactPrefs carries
    a comment saying as much, which is a note asking to be read rather than a
    control.

    Declaring a single <include> turns the rule from a denylist into an
    allowlist: shared_prefs/cardpro.xml is copied and nothing else is, now or
    after somebody adds a file here without thinking about backup. A card number
    written anywhere in this app's data directory stays on the device, which is
    what the "On-device" badge in the header promises.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="sharedpref" path="cardpro.xml" />
</full-backup-content>
